## 6.1.31 (2026.10.26 10:18:13)
- found diffed: com/killcoding/cache/CacheArray.java (M)
- TestCacheArray: rewritten to the task-driven three-consoles pattern
  (console_A setTask(cacheTaskInsert.js)+setTaskTimer('1ms')+taskExecute ->
  inserts exactly 1000 test_user rows once; console_B
  setTask(cacheTaskFind.js)+setTaskTimer('1s')+taskExecute -> finds the
  remaining rows into console_C's globalCacheArray every second; console_C
  setTask(cacheTaskCallback.js)+taskExecute -> runs once (default 0ms timer)
  and registers Console.callback; the filter worker then deletes every
  consumed row via getObject()). Assertion: count == 0, 1/1 PASS.
- test/: added task files cacheTaskInsert.js, cacheTaskFind.js,
  cacheTaskCallback.js.
- TestAll.js: suite list now shows TestIdentityCard (7 suites in total).
- Known issue (engine): TestRemoteFile copyTo/moveTo/writeToDisk are
  intermittently FAIL (multi-part merge keeps only part 0 - 16 bytes instead
  of 22). Root cause: RemoteFile.merge() binds rows.filter() before
  executor.find() fills the rows; when the JDBC fill is slower than the
  CacheArray worker, the worker can hit a null head and mark completed
  before every part row is queued. Tried "find first, filter later" - but
  CacheArray.add() drops values while filter == null, so merge never ran;
  reverted. Suggested engine fix: explicit fill-completed signalling in
  CacheArray (or merge consuming synchronously). Baseline: 84/88.


